# EnergiBox topic ACLs.
#
# Topic layout:
#   energibox/<mac>/consumption   device -> backend   power samples
#   energibox/<mac>/status        device -> backend   relay state
#   energibox/<mac>/control       backend -> device   ON / OFF
#
# The backend consumes telemetry and issues commands; devices do the exact
# opposite. Splitting the two accounts means a compromised device cannot
# switch other people's relays — the worst it can do is lie about itself.

# The backend: read everything, command anything.
user energibox-backend
topic read energibox/+/consumption
topic read energibox/+/status
topic write energibox/+/control

# Devices: publish their own telemetry, receive their own commands.
#
# This shared account still lets one device impersonate another, because
# they all authenticate as the same user. To close that off, give each
# device its own username and use the %u substitution below, which
# Mosquitto expands to the connecting username:
#
#   user energibox-device-AABBCCDDEEFF
#   topic write energibox/%u/consumption
#
# That requires provisioning per-device credentials in the firmware, so the
# shared account is the pragmatic starting point.
user energibox-device
topic write energibox/+/consumption
topic write energibox/+/status
topic read energibox/+/control

# Anyone not listed above gets nothing. There is no default-allow rule here
# and that is deliberate.
